{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '1,270p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoJing: Backend-Independent CBOR Storage\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: landed on master (2026-09-25, merge e149aa31). Architecture-reviewed\n     4\t2026-09-17 (`collab/1789680000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor.*`): no\n     5\tblocking findings, one medium-severity ambiguity (file-backend frame\n     6\townership, corrected below), and one cross-cutting change (the\n     7\t`dao.space.index`/`query` comparator changes) flagged as needing explicit\n     8\tsign-off from `dao.space`'s owners before this plan is built.\n     9\t\n    10\tRelated: [DaoJing](dao.jing.md), [DaoJing DHT](dao.jing.dht.md),\n    11\t[architectural foundations](datom.world.md).\n    12\t\n    13\t## Objective and invariant\n    14\t\n    15\tDaoJing is a backend-independent storage substrate, like Datomic storage.\n    16\tIt can store content in memory, a file, PostgreSQL, S3, or another backend.\n    17\t**This invariant must be preserved. CBOR is the stored data format, not a\n    18\tchoice of storage backend.**\n    19\t\n    20\tReplace Jing's handwritten hash encoding and EDN persistence with canonical\n    21\tCBOR bytes. Jing owns the value-to-bytes contract, content addressing, and\n    22\tintegrity verification. Backends store and retrieve opaque bytes by address\n    23\tand provide their own durability guarantees. They need no knowledge of CBOR,\n    24\tClojure values, datoms, indexes, or manifests. **One exception, narrower\n    25\tthan \"backend\" vs. \"not a backend\":** `dao.jing.file` is the only backend\n    26\tthat re-ingests its own output across process death — it must persist an\n    27\tappend-only log and later replay it with no external message boundary to\n    28\tmark records, so it alone must invent a self-describing frame, which is\n    29\twhy that frame is itself a CBOR structure (a two-element\n    30\t`[digest payload-bytes]` array; see *Memory and files*). `dao.jing.mem`\n    31\tnever persists, so it has no framing at all; `dao.jing.content` (formerly\n    32\t`dao.jing.remote`)/`dao.jing.dht`\n    33\tget record boundaries from the channel codec's own message framing, one\n    34\tapplication value per message: Transit-JSON text or canonical CBOR binary\n    35\ton a WebSocket channel, canonical CBOR on the UDP channel\n    36\t(`dao.stream.remote.md` 3.1, 3.2), one Transit-JSON value per DHT datagram.\n    37\tSo payload bytes travel as Base64 in the application value, never a raw\n    38\tCBOR array. This is not\n    39\tabout `dao.jing.file` being more or less \"Jing's own\" than any other\n    40\tbackend in this source tree — all of `memory`/`file`/`remote`/`dht` are\n    41\tequally in-repo; only PostgreSQL and S3 are genuinely third-party and not\n    42\tyet built. What is third-party-implementable is the byte-store *contract*\n    43\titself, not any particular shipped implementation of it. The exception is\n    44\tthat `dao.jing.file` parses its own frame with Jing's shared codec, never a\n    45\tdivergent per-backend codec or value interpretation.\n    46\t\n    47\t```text\n    48\twrite: value -> canonical CBOR bytes -> multihash address -> backend\n    49\tread:  address -> backend bytes -> integrity verification -> decoded value\n    50\t\n    51\tbackend: memory | file | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT | ...\n    52\t```\n    53\t\n    54\tThe multihash address is `:segment/<algorithm-id>-<lowercase-hex-digest>`,\n    55\tper [`dao.jing.hash-registry.md`](dao.jing.hash-registry.md). BLAKE3 is the\n    56\tdefault minting algorithm; SHA-256 remains an equally supported, explicitly\n    57\tselectable peer. Both algorithms hash the same canonical CBOR bytes for a\n    58\tgiven value; this document's byte contract is unaffected by which algorithm\n    59\tnames the resulting address.\n    60\t\n    61\tPostgreSQL and S3 illustrate the required interchangeability; implementing\n    62\tnew PostgreSQL or S3 backends is outside this codec migration.\n    63\t\n    64\t## Community precedent and integration choice\n    65\t\n    66\tReuse [Boring](https://github.com/replikativ/boring), the CBOR serializer built\n    67\tfor Datahike. Konserve integrates it as a pluggable JVM/JavaScript serializer\n    68\t(id 3), but marks it BETA, \"Not yet recommended for production stores\";\n    69\tFressian remains its default (verified against\n    70\t[Konserve serializers](https://github.com/replikativ/konserve/blob/main/src/konserve/serializers.cljc),\n    71\t2026-09-16).\n    72\tJing will reuse serialization directly without adopting Konserve's storage\n    73\tAPI, mutable document operations, or Datahike-specific type handlers.\n    74\t\n    75\tPin `org.replikativ/boring` to `0.1.30` on JVM and JavaScript, verified as the\n    76\tlatest release on 2026-09-16 against\n    77\t[Clojars](https://clojars.org/org.replikativ/boring). Implement the same\n    78\tJing-supported representation on Dart. Boring's upstream\n    79\t[compatibility policy](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/doc/COMPATIBILITY.md)\n    80\tpreserves released tag meanings and older-output readability, but does not\n    81\tpromise identical encoding bytes across releases (verified against that\n    82\tpolicy, 2026-09-16). Jing's pin and frozen byte/hash fixtures own byte\n    83\tstability; Konserve's integration does not. Dependency upgrades must pass\n    84\tthose fixtures without regeneration.\n    85\t\n    86\tSelect `:canonical` and explicitly set encode options for stringref off,\n    87\tshapes off, and no index frame. Verified against the\n    88\t[Boring README](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/README.md),\n    89\t2026-09-16: `:clojure` defaults to string deduplication and preserved float\n    90\twidth, `:canonical` uses bytewise key ordering and shortest numeric forms,\n    91\tand `:canonical-rfc7049` uses length-first ordering. Option precedence over\n    92\tprofile defaults is unverified; fixtures must prove the effective options,\n    93\trather than relying on profile selection alone.\n    94\t\n    95\t## Layering and interfaces\n    96\t\n    97\t- Introduce `dao.jing.cbor/encode` and `decode`, operating on host byte\n    98\t  arrays. Share normalization and validation across hosts; isolate Dart's\n    99\t  codec implementation behind the same interface.\n   100\t- Preserve the public value-facing `materialize!`, `get`, `segment-key`,\n   101\t  `content-hash`, and `close!` operations. Existing callers still write and\n   102\t  read values.\n   103\t- Introduce an explicit byte-store boundary below those operations:\n   104\t  `{:put-bytes-fn f :get-bytes-fn g :close-fn c}`. Put takes an address and\n   105\t  canonical payload bytes and returns `:inserted` or `:present`; get takes\n   106\t  an address and caller-supplied not-found sentinel and returns bytes or\n   107\t  that sentinel. A shared Jing wrapper exposes the existing content-store\n   108\t  handle over this byte store.\n   109\t- This contract is effect-payload-shaped (address, bytes → verdict), so it\n   110\t  can become the deferred effect-stream write path in `dao.jing.md` without\n   111\t  a contract break; the synchronous function handle is its present-day\n   112\t  shape, not a commitment. The put half is effect-shaped as stated; the get\n   113\t  half becomes effect-shaped once its outcome is the explicit\n   114\t  `{:found? boolean, :bytes b}` envelope — `dao.jing.remote` already\n   115\t  answers the `{:found? boolean, :value v}` shape this generalizes\n   116\t  (`remote.cljc:55-60,84-85`; `:bytes` is this plan's future key, not\n   117\t  `:value`, since the value here is a byte representation, not a decoded\n   118\t  Clojure value) — with bytes in a portable byte representation — the\n   119\t  caller-supplied not-found sentinel is a wrapper-level convenience over\n   120\t  that envelope, not part of the contract's streamable shape.\n   121\t- Encode once before a write, derive the address from those bytes, and pass\n   122\t  those same bytes to the backend. On `:present`, read back and verify the\n   123\t  bytes by hash and byte-for-byte equality with the proposed payload. Never\n   124\t  overwrite conflicting content. Return an address only after\n   125\t  the backend reports its applicable storage guarantee.\n   126\t- The wrapper derives and verifies addresses; a backend re-verifies a put\n   127\t  exactly when bytes arrive from an untrusted party, such as a remote/DHT\n   128\t  server checking a client's claim.\n   129\t- Verify every retrieved payload against its requested address before\n   130\t  decoding exactly one supported value. Canonicality verification (decode,\n   131\t  re-encode, byte-compare) occurs exactly once at each ingress of bytes Jing\n   132\t  did not encode itself: remote/DHT receipt and file replay acceptance,\n   133\t  before storage, caching, or exposure. Ordinary reads of accepted snapshots\n   134\t  hash-verify and decode without re-encoding. Validation belongs to the\n   135\t  shared codec/wrapper at those boundaries, not to backend value handlers;\n   136\t  immutable local forwarding does not repeat ingress validation. Missing\n   137\t  content remains distinct from stored nil, which has a CBOR representation.\n   138\t- Store immutable snapshots. Memory backends copy byte arrays on insertion\n   139\t  and retrieval so caller mutation cannot change previously addressed\n   140\t  content. Other backends must provide the same observable isolation.\n   141\t- Keep backend handles and composition explicit. Introduce no global\n   142\t  backend registry, ambient codec options, or domain-specific interpretation.\n   143\t\n   144\t## Encoding contract\n   145\t\n   146\tUse Boring's `:canonical` profile with string references, shaped arrays, and\n   147\toffset indexes disabled. Jing's normalization and named CBOR extensions are\n   148\tfixed, not backend-configurable. The hash covers only canonical payload\n   149\tbytes, never a backend coordinate, file frame, address envelope, or network\n   150\tmessage.\n   151\t\n   152\tSupported values are nil, booleans, strings, keywords, symbols, byte strings,\n   153\tvectors, finite lists/sequences, maps, sets, metadata, integers,\n   154\tfloating-point numbers, big integers, decimals, and ratios. \"Supported\" is\n   155\tqualified: it means the encoder accepts the value once its metadata is\n   156\thost-clean. On Dart, a runtime-created list can carry an unsupported\n   157\tconstructor `:tag` (a Dart Type), and internal producers such as the VM's\n   158\treader-position projection can reattach such metadata. Reject arbitrary\n   159\trecords, functions, and unsupported host objects before storage. The\n   160\tnumeric carriers described below are explicitly supported exceptions to\n   161\tthe record restriction; arbitrary Boring records do not become Jing\n   162\tvalues. **This is\n   163\ta narrower domain than today's transitional `pr-str`-based encoder, which\n   164\taddresses anything printable/readable** — characters, `#inst`, `#uuid`, and\n   165\tother tagged literals are addressable today and have no slot in this\n   166\tsupported-values list or in Jing's four named CBOR extensions; they become\n   167\trejected, unsupported values once this plan lands. No current producer\n   168\temits them, so nothing observed breaks, but \"existing stores... must be\n   169\trebuilt together\" (*Addressing and clean break*) presumes every stored\n   170\tvalue is re-encodable under the new domain — an assumption this delta\n   171\tshould be checked against before rebuild, not discovered during it.\n   172\t\n   173\t- Normalize sorted collections to ordinary maps and sets. Sort map keys\n   174\t  and set elements lexicographically by unsigned canonical encoded bytes\n   175\t  (a proper prefix sorts first), not length-first. Reject duplicate canonical\n   176\t  keys or elements introduced by normalization instead of losing entries.\n   177\t  Supported maps/sets must also survive portable decoded equality without\n   178\t  collapsing entries: reject such collisions on encode and before decoded\n   179\t  collection construction, including numerically equal keys of different\n   180\t  kinds. Do not let the accepting host determine this boundary.\n   181\t- Encode strings as UTF-8 without Unicode normalization. Reject unpaired\n   182\t  high or low surrogates before encoding on every host, including in\n   183\t  identifier components and metadata; normal astral text and noncharacters\n   184\t  remain legal. Reject invalid UTF-8 on decode. Boring's own surrogate\n   185\t  pre-validation is unverified; Jing owns this rule regardless.\n   186\t- Lists and sequences share a representation distinct from vectors. Use\n   187\t  CBOR tag 27 with the name `dao.jing/list` and an array argument; plain\n   188\t  CBOR arrays represent vectors.\n   189\t- Preserve collection and symbol metadata using Boring's `clojure/with-meta`\n   190\t  mapping. Strip `:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, and `:end-column`; omit empty\n   191\t  metadata. Clear metadata on internally constructed Dart collections\n   192\t  before attaching supplied metadata. Do not strip arbitrary user `:tag`\n   193\t  metadata as a workaround for Dart constructor metadata. This clearing\n   194\t  covers only collections Jing constructs at decode: a Dart producer\n   195\t  building a payload with `list` must still clear ClojureDart's constructor\n   196\t  metadata itself (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`), and so must internal\n   197\t  producers reconstructing lists in Jing-bound code, such as the de Bruijn\n   198\t  projection; that `dao.jing.md` open item carries forward, it is not\n   199\t  retired by this plan.\n   200\t- Encode **all** keywords and symbols as tag 27 with `dao.jing/keyword` or\n   201\t  `dao.jing/symbol` and `[namespace name]`, where namespace is nil or a\n   202\t  string and name is a string, taken directly from the identifier's fields.\n   203\t  Uniform escaping removes a host-sensitive ordinary/escaped predicate.\n   204\t  Never parse or print these components to reconstruct an identifier;\n   205\t  preserve nil versus empty namespace and literal slashes, whitespace, and\n   206\t  leading colons. Verified against\n   207\t  [Boring's writer](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/src/boring/writer.cljs),\n   208\t  2026-09-16: its native tag 39 carries slash-joined text with a keyword\n   209\t  colon prefix, so distinct namespace/name pairs or colon-leading symbols\n   210\t  can collide. Jing has no native-identifier arm: reject tag 39, including\n   211\t  otherwise ordinary names, and enforce that same boundary by canonical\n   212\t  re-encoding at ingress.\n   213\t- Hash byte strings by content, independent of host object identity.\n   214\t- Reject malformed CBOR, unknown tags or named wrappers outside this\n   215\t  profile, duplicate map keys or set elements, trailing data, and\n   216\t  unsupported decoded values. Do not enable lossy encoder fallbacks.\n   217\t\n   218\tJing's four names use tag 27 followed by exactly `[name-string, payload]`:\n   219\t`dao.jing/list` takes an array, `dao.jing/keyword` and `dao.jing/symbol` take\n   220\tthe two-component arrays above, and `dao.jing/float64` takes an eight-byte\n   221\tbyte string. Tag 27's object meaning is verified against the\n   222\t[IANA registry](https://www.iana.org/assignments/cbor-tags/cbor-tags.xhtml);\n   223\tthe array-payload grammar and public mechanisms are verified against\n   224\t[Boring extension documentation](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/doc/EXTENDING.md)\n   225\tand [records.cljc](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/src/boring/records.cljc),\n   226\t2026-09-16. Write these frames with `tagged-literal`; read them as\n   227\t`boring.data/UnknownRecord`, then convert recursively through a closed Jing\n   228\tname/shape dispatch before exposing values, preserving validated metadata\n   229\twhen converting a wrapper to its value. Use no ambient registry or\n   230\trecord/map constructor macros for these array payloads. Other UnknownRecords\n   231\tare errors. This chosen post-decode path deliberately does not set\n   232\t`:on-unknown-record :error`, which would reject Jing's own names; that\n   233\toption alone would not close numeric tags anyway.\n   234\t\n   235\tExplicitly reject inert `boring.data/TaggedValue` and every `SimpleValue`\n   236\toutside Jing's nil/boolean domain: unknown numeric tags survive Boring\n   237\tdecoding as TaggedValue (verified against\n   238\t[data.cljc](https://github.com/replikativ/boring/blob/main/src/boring/data.cljc),\n   239\t2026-09-16). Validate tag/name/payload shapes before any lossy host\n   240\tconversion; successful generic decoding is not profile acceptance. Native\n   241\tCBOR floating-point items are forbidden even if a host decoder could turn\n   242\tone into an integer. Frozen fixtures define the remaining Boring mappings\n   243\tfor sets and `clojure/with-meta`, whose payload is `[meta value]`; no other\n   244\tBoring collection or record mappings are admitted. All lengths and integer\n   245\targuments use shortest definite encodings; integers fitting CBOR major\n   246\ttypes 0/1 do not use bignum tags, and bignum magnitudes have no leading zero.\n   247\t\n   248\t### Ingress limits and host-capability refusals\n   249\t\n   250\tRatified from the J0-J3 fixture corpus and its errata\n   251\t(`test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.errata.md`), not derived from the text\n   252\tabove: an implementation must enforce these explicitly rather than rely on\n   253\thost defaults.\n   254\t\n   255\t- Cap CBOR item nesting depth at 128 on both encode and decode, counting the\n   256\t  top-level item as depth 1 and every array, map, and tag as one level\n   257\t  deeper (a `dao.jing/list` frame therefore costs three levels). Refuse\n   258\t  `:unsupported-value` on encode and `:malformed-cbor` on decode past the\n   259\t  cap; this is a robustness rule against unbounded recursion, independent\n   260\t  of the profile's other refusal classes.\n   261\t- Constrain a decimal's exponent to `[-(2^31 - 1), 2^31]`, the JVM\n   262\t  `BigDecimal` scale range, identically on every host. Refuse\n   263\t  `:malformed-number` outside it. Without a shared window, one host can\n   264\t  accept a canonical payload another host refuses, letting the accepting\n   265\t  host determine the boundary the contract otherwise forbids.\n   266\t- A host whose identifier equality is joined-name-based (ClojureScript\n   267\t  keywords and symbols compare by namespace/name joined into one string)\n   268\t  cannot hold two Jing values that are namespace/name-distinct but\n   269\t  joined-name-equal (for example `(keyword nil \"a/b\")` and\n   270\t  `(keyword \"a\" \"b\")`) in one decoded map or set without merging them. This\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
