{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The codec already has a float64 carrier specifically to preserve integral floats on JavaScript. Its bare-number classification conforms to that contract; the defect is upstream, where source float literals reach addressed rows without their kind. I’m checking how to preserve that kind without changing runtime arithmetic or existing canonical bytes.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '387,439p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc | sed -n '262,292p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc | sed -n '66,97p'; rg -n 'float|literal|:value|number' src/cljc/yin/reader.cljc src/cljc/yin/db.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '1840,1862p'; rg -n 'float-free|golden|float64' test/yang/*/*safepoint* test/yang/*safepoint* test/dao/jing/* 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '387,439p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc | sed -n '262,292p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc | sed -n '66,97p'; rg -n 'float|literal|:value|number' src/cljc/yin/reader.cljc src/cljc/yin/db.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '1840,1862p'; rg -n 'float-free|golden|float64' test/yang/*/*safepoint* test/yang/*safepoint* test/dao/jing/* 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"   387\t- Provide a portable `float64` constructor/carrier. JavaScript callers use\n   388\t  it for integral floating-point values such as `1.0`; ordinary integral\n   389\t  JavaScript numbers remain integers, except native negative zero, which\n   390\t  is floating-point content so its sign survives. Decoding floating-point content on\n   391\t  JavaScript returns the carrier so re-encoding cannot lose its kind.\n   392\t- Encode floating-point content on every host as tag 27,\n   393\t  `dao.jing/float64`, with eight big-endian IEEE-754 bytes. This avoids\n   394\t  Boring's native-number differences between JVM and JavaScript, verified\n   395\t  against its compatibility policy above: integral JS numbers encode as\n   396\t  integers while JVM `1.0` remains a float under `:canonical`.\n   397\t  Widen native float32 values to float64, preserve signed zero and\n   398\t  infinities, and canonicalize NaNs to quiet NaN bits `0x7ff8000000000000`.\n   399\t  **The Jing profile emits no native CBOR floating-point whatsoever**;\n   400\t  Boring's canonical float narrowing and NaN encoding never govern Jing\n   401\t  floating-point bytes. The carrier is Jing-owned; upstream has no float64\n   402\t  wrapper (verified against Boring `data.cljc`, 2026-09-16).\n   403\t- Use CBOR integers and bignum tags 2/3 for exact integers. Never convert\n   404\t  large integers through a floating-point intermediate. Reject unsafe\n   405\t  integral JavaScript Number inputs; callers must supply BigInt for exact\n   406\t  integers or the float64 carrier for floating-point content.\n   407\t- Use decimal-fraction tag 4 with `[exponent mantissa]`; preserve decimal\n   408\t  scale. Use rational tag 30 with a reduced numerator and positive,\n   409\t  nonzero denominator. Retain rational kind even when the denominator is 1.\n   410\t  Tags 2/3, 4, and 30 are registered meanings, verified against IANA above,\n   411\t  2026-09-16; Jing allocates no custom numeric tag.\n   412\t- Use native exact numeric types where they preserve the declared kind and\n   413\t  representation; otherwise use immutable carriers. Reuse Boring's\n   414\t  JavaScript decimal/rational representations, verified against Boring\n   415\t  `data.cljc`, 2026-09-16: `Decimal [exponent mantissa]` and\n   416\t  `Rational [numerator denominator]` exist on JVM and CLJS, with Rational\n   417\t  re-encoding as tag 30. Wrap/adapt them as needed for the portable numeric\n   418\t  operations above. Dart uses BigInt and explicit\n   419\t  decimal/rational carriers. Add portable constructors and accessors;\n   420\t  extending VM arithmetic is outside this migration.\n   421\t\n   422\t## Backend changes\n   423\t\n   424\tAll existing backends move to the shared byte-store boundary. No backend\n   425\tselects a different value codec or re-hashes a host-specific representation\n   426\t— with one named exception, `dao.jing.file`'s own frame parsing (see the\n   427\tObjective's carve-out, and *Memory and files* below).\n   428\t\n   429\t### Memory and files\n   430\t\n   431\tThe memory backend stores byte snapshots under content addresses. The file\n   432\tbackend retains its append-only length framing, durability rules, duplicate\n   433\tvalidation, and torn-tail recovery. Each frame contains a CBOR\n   434\t`[digest payload-bytes]` record: digest is the raw digest byte string for\n   435\tthe address's carried algorithm (both initial registry algorithms are 32\n   436\tbytes) and payload-bytes contains the exact canonical payload.\n   437\t**`dao.jing.file` itself owns parsing and constructing this two-element\n   438\tframe**, with Jing's own shared codec — the exception the Objective names\n   439\tabove, needed because `dao.jing.file`'s frame shape is itself a CBOR\n   262\t                (when (> x max-exact-int)\n   263\t                  (unsupported! n \"integer literal beyond 2^53\"))\n   264\t                x)))\n   265\t          0\n   266\t          digits))\n   267\t\n   268\t\n   269\t(defn- parse-number\n   270\t  [n text]\n   271\t  (let [t (str/replace text \"_\" \"\")\n   272\t        lower (str/lower-case t)]\n   273\t    (cond\n   274\t      (str/ends-with? lower \"j\") (unsupported! n \"imaginary literal\")\n   275\t      (str/starts-with? lower \"0x\") (parse-radix n (subs t 2) 16)\n   276\t      (str/starts-with? lower \"0o\") (parse-radix n (subs t 2) 8)\n   277\t      (str/starts-with? lower \"0b\") (parse-radix n (subs t 2) 2)\n   278\t      ;; floats are tagged on every host (owner decision 3)\n   279\t      (re-find #\"[.eE]\" t) {:py/float #?(:cljd (double/parse t)\n   280\t                                         :clj (Double/parseDouble t)\n   281\t                                         :cljs (js/parseFloat t))}\n   282\t      ;; Python 3 reads 0755 as an error, not as octal or decimal\n   283\t      (and (str/starts-with? t \"0\") (re-find #\"[1-9]\" t))\n   284\t      (syntax! n (str \"leading zeros in decimal integer literals are not \"\n   285\t                      \"permitted; use an 0o prefix for octal integers\"))\n   286\t      :else (parse-radix n t 10))))\n   287\t\n   288\t\n   289\t(defn- code-point->str\n   290\t  [cp]\n   291\t  #?(:cljd (let [sb (StringBuffer)] (.writeCharCode sb cp) (.toString sb))\n   292\t     :clj (String. (Character/toChars (int cp)))\n    66\t   (doubling division, binary-descent floor, two's-complement recursion),\n    67\t   valid within 2^53.\n    68\t\n    69\t   Host names the prelude depends on and does not define: `host-names`\n    70\t   (the cell module from yin.vm.module, the rest from yin.vm.data).\"\n    71\t  (:require\n    72\t    [yang.python.antlr.uast :as u]))\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\t(def host-names\n    76\t  \"Module exports the prelude calls.\"\n    77\t  '#{cell/new cell/get cell/set! data/count data/into data/subvec\n    78\t     data/number? data/dissoc data/str-concat data/str-length\n    79\t     data/str-index-of data/str->code-points data/code-points->str})\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(def ^:private core-definitions\n    83\t  \"Pure definitions: lambdas only, so defining them runs nothing.\"\n    84\t  '[;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- kinds\n    85\t    [py/cell? (fn [x] (= (get x :type) :cell-ref))]\n    86\t    [py/str? (fn [x] (not (nil? (get x :py/str))))]\n    87\t    [py/str (fn [s] (assoc {} :py/str s))]\n    88\t    [py/float? (fn [x] (not (nil? (get x :py/float))))]\n    89\t    [py/float (fn [x] (assoc {} :py/float (* 1.0 x)))]\n    90\t    [py/tuple (fn [items] (assoc (assoc {} :py/type :tuple) :items items))]\n    91\t    [py/conj (fn [xs x] (conj xs x))]\n    92\t    [py/arg (fn [args i] (get args i))]\n    93\t    [py/numeric?\n    94\t     (fn [x]\n    95\t       (if (= x true)\n    96\t         true\n    97\t         (if (= x false) true (if (py/float? x) true (data/number? x)))))]\nrg: src/cljc/yin/reader.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nrg: src/cljc/yin/db.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\n  1840\t+--------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1841\t| Slice  | Scope                                                               |\n  1842\t+========+=====================================================================+\n  1843\t| S1     | Core: `py/make-generator`, `py/gen-switch`, `py/yield`,             |\n  1844\t|        | `py/gen-exit`, `py/gen-fail`; `StopIteration`; `next`; the          |\n  1845\t|        | `iter-at` and `iterable` arms; lowering arms for `yield_stmt` and   |\n  1846\t|        | `yield_expr`, a `:gen` binder reset in every nested scope, and      |\n  1847\t|        | removal of the three `yield` guards.                                |\n  1848\t+--------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1849\t| S2     | `send`, `throw`, `close`, and the dynamic context: `GeneratorExit`, |\n  1850\t|        | `finally` and `with` around a yield, the \"generator already         |\n  1851\t|        | executing\" check, PEP 479.                                          |\n  1852\t+--------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1853\t| S3     | `yield from`, `iter`, and stateful sequence iterators.              |\n  1854\t+--------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1855\t| S4     | Generator expressions, with removal of the C1 consuming-builtin     |\n  1856\t|        | inlining.                                                           |\n  1857\t+--------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1858\t| S5     | Heap, wire, and hosts: collection of suspended and dropped          |\n  1859\t|        | generators, lift refusal, determinism, snapshot rendering;          |\n  1860\t|        | user-defined iterator classes.                                      |\n  1861\t+--------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1862\t```\nzsh:1: no matches found: test/yang/*/*safepoint*\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
